@meta
id: 30-08
title: Keeping the index fresh
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: ctags
par: 112
minutes: 7
vscode: nothing — the extension reindexes and you never think about it
open: .vimrc .gitignore

@brief
A tags file is a snapshot. Rename a method and the index still points at
the old line until something rebuilds it. Nobody does that by hand twice.

Two small pieces of setup, and you never think about it again.

  1. In `.vimrc`, after the existing settings, two lines:

         set tags=./tags;,tags
         nnoremap <F5> :!ctags -R --exclude=node_modules src<CR>

  2. In `.gitignore`, a third entry: `tags`. It is generated, it is
     large, and it changes on every commit.

Type them exactly as written. `ctags` is not installed in the dojo, so
this lesson is the configuration rather than the run — but the two lines
are the ones you would put in a real `.vimrc` tonight.

@keys
| `set tags=./tags;,tags` | look for `tags` beside the file, then upward to the root |
| `:!cmd` | run a shell command without leaving vim |
| `nnoremap` | a normal-mode mapping that does not expand other mappings |
| `:wn` | write this file and move to the next one in the argument list |

@hints
Two files are open. `:wn` writes the first and moves to the second;
`:wqa` at the end writes and quits everything.
--
`G` then `o` opens a line under the last one. Autoindent has nothing to
copy here, so what you type is what you get.
--
`G o` the two lines `Esc` `:wn` `G o tags Esc :wqa`.

@solution
    G o set tags=./tags;,tags<Enter>nnoremap <F5> :!ctags -R --exclude=node_modules src<CR> <Esc>
    :wn<Enter>  G o tags <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

The semicolon in `./tags;` is the part worth understanding. `./tags`
means "a tags file in the same directory as the file being edited".
The `;` means "and if it is not there, keep looking upward until you hit
the root". So one tags file at the top of the repo serves every file in
it, and a per-package one closer to the file wins when it exists. That
single option is what makes the whole scheme usable in a monorepo.

The mapping is the crude version. The next step up is rebuilding on
save:

    autocmd BufWritePost *.ts silent! !ctags -R --exclude=node_modules src &

with the `&` to background it, and `silent!` so a missing `ctags` never
interrupts you. Bigger repos usually run it from a git hook instead —
`post-merge` and `post-checkout` are the two that matter, because that
is when a lot of code changes underneath you at once.

Committing the tags file is the classic mistake. It is derived, it is
often megabytes, and it conflicts on every merge. Generate it; ignore it.

@start .vimrc
set number
set ignorecase smartcase
set hidden

@expected .vimrc
set number
set ignorecase smartcase
set hidden
set tags=./tags;,tags
nnoremap <F5> :!ctags -R --exclude=node_modules src<CR>

@start .gitignore
node_modules/
dist/

@expected .gitignore
node_modules/
dist/
tags
